WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.230
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. So I've

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.399
been thinking a lot about the world's

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.260
largest ball of paint, which is located

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.280
in the tiny town of Alexandria, Indiana.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.660
Sidenote: it can't possibly be a

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.280
coincidence that so many of the world's

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.220
largest balls are located in the United States,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.920
like the world's largest ball of popcorn is in Iowa.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.600
The world's largest ball of stamps is in Nebraska.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.320
There are several competing world's largest balls

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.080
of twine from Kansas to Minnesota, but

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.740
it's the ball of paint that interests me most.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.880
So back in 1977, this guy, Mike Carmichael,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.120
painted a baseball with his three years old son

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.860
and then they kept painting it for like hundreds of coats.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.240
And then they started to invite neighbors and friends over to paint the ball.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.800
Eventually strangers showed up and

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.380
Mike would invite them to add layers of paint,

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.350
and now 40 years later, there are

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.040
more than 25,000 layers of paint on this baseball.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.600
It weighs two and a half tons.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.500
It has its own little house and

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.220
every year, more than a thousand strangers show

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.140
up to add layers of paint to it, which by the way is free!

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.300
Mike even provides the paint.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.400
Anyway, I think the world's largest ball of paint

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.380
has something to tell us about the creation of art.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.540
Like usually, we imagine art as a story of individual geniuses, right?

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.660
You know, Shakespeare, Leonardo da Vinci,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.820
or whomever uses, like,

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.920
their innate brilliance to expand the human landscape.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.380
Now, sometimes, we acknowledge that

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.700
circumstances play a role in the emergence of great art.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.560
Like, for instance, for Mark Twain to become Mark Twain,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.780
he had to grow up on the river that separated America from itself

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.960
during the war that separated America from itself.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.440
But mostly, we think the work is done not by the time,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.720
but by these heroic individuals, who, like, push us forward and lifts us up.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.500
Now, to be clear, I do believe in genius --

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.140
from Mark Twain to Jane Austen to Toni Morrison.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.820
There are some artists who are just uncommonly great.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.900
But I don't think genius is a simple trait, like having brown eyes.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.360
I think it's more of a continuum, like having empathy.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.280
And also, I think the worship of individual genius, in art and elsewhere, is ultimately misguided.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.680
Like Thomas Edison didn't invent the lightbulb,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.560
it was being invented all around him by lots of people

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.200
and he added innovations to it.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.340
And Isaac Newton didn't discover gravity,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.600
he pushed our awareness of it to new places.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.860
I think lots of us, and I include myself in this by the way,

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.340
worry that whatever our work is, it won't matter because it won't be remembered

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.580
or because it won't rise to the level of "genius".

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.580
But maybe that makes individuals too important.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.740
Maybe, in the end, art and life are more like the world's largest ball of paint.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.260
You carefully choose your colors,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.240
and then you add your layer as best you can,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.360
and then, eventually, it gets painted over.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.580
It gets painted again and again,

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.500
until there is no visible remnant of your layer of paint,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.020
and really no one knows about it,

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:25.000
except for you and maybe a few people close to you to whom you told the story.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.300
But that doesn't mean your layer of paint is irrelevant.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.940
Because it permanently, if slightly, changed the larger sphere.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.220
Right, like, the world's largest ball of paint looks nothing like a painted baseball

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.560
and you're part of the reason --

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.080
whether you made a YouTube video that only a few people saw

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.560
or wrote a short story that only a few people read --

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.380
you still played a role in shaping this many splendored ball of paint we're all living with.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.160
So you add your coat of paint, or hopefully several coats,

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.880
and hope that you've made the world more beautiful.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.140
And yes, eventually, you will be painted over, but you still matter.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.040
Hank, thanks so much to you and all the VidCon staff and volunteers

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.700
for an amazing weekend in Amsterdam at VidCon Europe.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.360
I'm going to enjoy a couple days of vacation

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.900
and then get back to painting.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.260
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

